import{_ as o,C as t,o as n,c as i,aB as r,E as s}from"./chunks/framework.BPloFSFy.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"PSII excitation and fluorescence","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"fluorescence.md","filePath":"fluorescence.md","lastUpdated":null}'),c={name:"fluorescence.md"};function l(d,e,p,h,u,m){const a=t("LeafCharts");return n(),i("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=r('<h1 id="PSII-excitation-and-fluorescence" tabindex="-1">PSII excitation and fluorescence <a class="header-anchor" href="#PSII-excitation-and-fluorescence" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;PSII excitation and fluorescence {#PSII-excitation-and-fluorescence}&quot;">​</a></h1><p><a href="https://github.com/RSFraLab/LeafPhotosynthesis.jl/blob/main/examples/fluorescence.jl" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Run the example</a> or open the <a href="/LeafPhotosynthesis.jl/dev/examples.html#gallery-fluorescence">offline plots</a>. TOML reading and component assembly live in <a href="https://github.com/RSFraLab/LeafPhotosynthesis.jl/blob/main/examples/setup/fluorescence.jl" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">setup</a>; all numerical choices are in <a href="https://github.com/RSFraLab/LeafPhotosynthesis.jl/blob/main/examples/parameters/fluorescence.toml" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">the parameter file</a>.</p><p>The example has fixed temperature and prescribed biochemical-site CO₂. It obtains PSII excitation Q₂ from the same <code>psii_fraction * absorbed_par</code> used by electron transport, and realized linear flow J from <code>biochemical_turnover</code>. J is not the potential electron supply and is not reconstructed from net carbon uptake. It remains finite at the photorespiratory compensation point. Optional O₂ diagnostics can remain disabled.</p><h2 id="Replaceable-components" tabindex="-1">Replaceable components <a class="header-anchor" href="#Replaceable-components" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Replaceable components {#Replaceable-components}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>RateCompetitionFluorescence &lt;: AbstractFluorescence</code> contains explicit relative rates and a concrete <code>AbstractQuenching{FT}</code> component. <code>ConstantQuenching</code> and <code>SaturationQuenching</code> provide two choices; another law can implement <code>quenching_rate(law, saturation)</code> without changing a switch or union. To use the host coupling check, also define <code>fluorescence_low_light_yield</code> for that law&#39;s rate-competition model, or supply a specialized coupling validator. The function <code>fluorescence(model, LeafPhotosynthesis.PSIIFluxInputs(Q₂, J))</code> returns an immutable scalar result. No field was added to <code>LeafModel</code>; callers compose this diagnostic alongside photosynthesis. <code>NoFluorescence</code> returns <code>nothing</code> without input preparation.</p><p>For lit populations, ΦP=J/Q₂ and the nonphotochemical remainder is divided among KF, KD and KN. Thus ΦF=(1−ΦP)KF/(KF+KD+KN), with analogous heat probabilities. The inferred KP=ΦP(KF+KD+KN)/(1−ΦP) is checked against the declared maximum. The four probabilities sum to one. They do <strong>not</strong> establish leaf joule conservation: Stokes loss, thermalization and chemical-energy storage require additional accounting.</p><p>The empirical option uses relative saturation x=1−ΦP/ΦPmax and the KN curve in van der Tol et al. (2014), Eq. 19. The example explicitly supplies the cotton fit, KF=.05, KD=.95 and KPmax=4, giving ΦPmax=.8. This is a constant-KD illustration; the paper also treats KD(T). Its PAM-derived fit has PSI-contamination limitations, so this PSII-only use is an approximation, not a validated pure-PSII calibration. All rate coefficients share a normalization; scaling every rate together leaves the probabilities unchanged. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1002/2014JG002713" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Primary paper</a></p><p>The new example supplies a compatible .8 low-light electron yield. Existing Bonan exercises retain .85. <code>validate_fluorescence_coupling</code> rejects an incompatible combination instead of retuning it. A constant positive KN requires its corresponding quenched low-light electron yield; the empirical option requires the unquenched maximum. Valid parameters can still be incompatible at a particular operating point, so runtime checks remain necessary.</p><h2 id="Darkness,-failure-and-floating-point" tabindex="-1">Darkness, failure and floating point <a class="header-anchor" href="#Darkness,-failure-and-floating-point" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Darkness, failure and floating point {#Darkness,-failure-and-floating-point}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>RateCompetitionResult{FT}</code> carries <code>FluorescenceStatus</code>, <code>boundary_roundoff</code>, internal photon flux, four yields and inferred rates. <code>is_valid</code> accepts lit and dark results. In exact darkness, Q₂=J=0, the computed photon flux is zero but flux-derived yields and rates are NaN: they are undefined, not measured zero. The example converts only these valid-dark yields to plot gaps and empty CSV cells. An invalid result has NaN flux and fails example/report generation.</p><p>Negative/nonfinite inputs, nonzero J with zero excitation, or incompatible maximum rates are explicit failures. Domain checks precede division and fractional powers. The parameter file supplies an eight-epsilon yield tolerance for FT32/FT64. A tolerated maximum-yield excess is flagged, preserving J/Q₂, inferred KP and emitted photons; only an infinitesimally negative saturation coordinate uses x=0 in KN. The quenched maximum is checked in yield units as well. ΦP≥1 is always rejected. The tolerance is a numerical boundary policy, not a tunable physiological allowance.</p><h2 id="Connection-to-other-models" tabindex="-1">Connection to other models <a class="header-anchor" href="#Connection-to-other-models" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Connection to other models {#Connection-to-other-models}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>fluorescence_inputs</code> accepts a biochemical-site <code>LeafEnvironment</code>. For solved gas exchange, first verify convergence, then construct that environment using accepted Cc (<code>solution.biochemical_co2</code>). Ambient CO₂ is not interchangeable with Cc; no ambient gas-environment overload is provided. A failed gas-exchange solve must not be passed off as a valid fluorescence source.</p><p>The scalar photon flux is internal <strong>PSII</strong> production, before optical escape. Spectral adapters must apply its yield once to the same PSII excitation bins, then account for leaf-face escape, reabsorption and canopy transport separately. This package contains no wavelength grid or spectral matrix dependency.</p><p>There is no prognostic NPQ memory, PSI emission, alternative sink or cyclic flow in this component. A future coupled photosystem family is free to use richer inputs and outputs instead of being forced through Q₂/J alone. Johnson &amp; Berry&#39;s model explicitly couples photosystems, Cyt-b6f and carbon metabolism, motivating that boundary. <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8292351/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Primary article</a></p><p>Tests cover independent high-precision rate reconstruction, compensation, dark/lit reordering, normalization invariance, incompatible rates, numerical boundaries, NoOp behavior, a downstream quenching extension, FT32/FT64 and CUDA execution.</p><div class="leaf-app" data-leaf-app="figures" data-figures="fluorescence-yield fluorescence-photons"></div>',17)),s(a)])}const b=o(c,[["render",l]]);export{g as __pageData,b as default};
